_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 15
---
order: 15
---
short_label: XII.15
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: In equal cones and cylinders the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights; and those cones and cylinders in which the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights are equal.
----
proof:

Let there be equal cones and cylinders with the circular bases {ABCD|circABCD} and {EFGH|circEFGH}. Let {AC} and {EG} be the diameters of the bases, and {KL|LK} and {MN} the axes, which are also the heights of the cones or cylinders

Complete the cylinders {AO|cylAO} and {EP|cylEP}.

I say that in the cylinders {AO|cylAO} and {EP|cylEP} the bases are reciprocally proportional to the heights, that is, the base {ABCD|circABCD} is to the base {EFGH|circEFGH} as the height {MN} is to the height {KL|LK}.

<center>
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="550" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XII.15 diagram" src="propXII15.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Two cases on the heights. Equal: XII.11 gives the bases equal. Unequal
    // (MN the greater): QN is cut off equal to KL, the plane TUS through Q
    // cuts off the cylinder ES; V.7 with XII.11 and XII.13, through V.11,
    // gives ABCD : EFGH :: MN : KL. The converse runs the same proportions
    // back to V.9. The cones follow by XII.10.
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws the cylinders AO and EP with the cones ACL and
